WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.800
Data cleaning.

2
00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:06.367
It is unglamorous, but it is where most real world model failures begin.

3
00:00:07.567 --> 00:00:11.867
Cleaning is everything you do before the data is wrong in interesting ways.

4
00:00:12.167 --> 00:00:13.733
Remove duplicate rows.

5
00:00:14.033 --> 00:00:16.267
Fix types, like numbers stored as text.

6
00:00:16.567 --> 00:00:17.915
Decide on outliers.

7
00:00:17.915 --> 00:00:22.100
Is a forty thousand kelvin star a typo, or a real hot star?

8
00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:24.800
And normalize inconsistent categories.

9
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.567
Here is a classic problem.

10
00:00:27.867 --> 00:00:33.133
Kepler, all caps KEPLER, and kepler with a trailing space look like one telescope to you.

11
00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:36.000
To a model, they are three separate categories.

12
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:39.867
Strip the spaces and lower the case, and they collapse into one.

13
00:00:41.067 --> 00:00:44.133
The professional habit is to treat cleaning as a script.

14
00:00:44.433 --> 00:00:46.200
The raw file stays untouched.

15
00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:49.000
Every fix is code that logs what it changed.

16
00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:54.767
So next month's data pull gets cleaned identically, and an auditor can see exactly what happened.

17
00:00:55.967 --> 00:00:57.700
Here are those fixes in pandas.

18
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.267
Drop duplicate planets, and print how many we removed.

19
00:01:01.567 --> 00:01:05.600
Strip the commas from temperature text and convert it to a real number.

20
00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:08.467
Then strip and lowercase the telescope names.

21
00:01:09.667 --> 00:01:11.500
Here is how it bites in practice.

22
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:16.833
After a catalog merge, the classifier treats KEPLER planets differently from Kepler ones.

23
00:01:17.133 --> 00:01:21.500
One-hot encoding gave each spelling its own column and its own learned weight.

24
00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:27.333
A three line cleaning fix recovered more accuracy than any hyperparameter tuned that week.

25
00:01:28.533 --> 00:01:29.363
Let's recap.

26
00:01:29.363 --> 00:01:32.267
Dedupe, fix types, and decide on outliers.

27
00:01:32.567 --> 00:01:34.133
Normalize your categories.

28
00:01:34.433 --> 00:01:36.433
Cleaning is a script, not a session.

29
00:01:36.733 --> 00:01:38.167
And log every fix.

30
00:01:38.167 --> 00:01:40.000
Next up: normalization.
